begin model
# =====================================================================
# FluBNF SIHRS — TWO-AGE-CLASS (children / adults) MIN — PF candidate
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Same trim as SIHRS_pop_min.bngl (the single-class model the PF actually
# runs): no impr, no semi-annual harmonic, single-season fits, the filter
# re-conditions weekly.
#
# HYPOTHESIS UNDER TEST
# ---------------------
# Paediatric admissions LEAD adult admissions, and the lead is internal to
# the target stream, so it is immune to the ED trap (a concurrent external
# correlate that only echoes the latent state). Measured before writing this
# model, on NHSN age strata 2024-25, full grid: paediatric admissions growth
# at week t predicts ADULT admissions growth at week t+1 with median partial
# correlation +0.315 after conditioning on two lags of adult growth (71% of
# 31 jurisdictions above +0.2, sign-consistent). The paediatric SHARE LEVEL
# is null on the same test (+0.09, sign-incoherent) -- the information is in
# the growth, which is what a two-class mechanism converts into an earlier
# turn without anyone specifying a growth statistic.
#
# Fitted (6): Reff, eps1, phi1, theta, mult, r   -- ONE more than production,
# against six new observables per week (the age strata).
#
# Tokens: 39431263 0.85 5.71134304e-05 2.187500 1.17 0.019
#         0.214226 2.0000 1.0000 0.0050227499 0.03439439 0.490078
#
# Channels: H_weekly (NHSN total admissions, NB)
#         + Ped_share (paediatric fraction of age-coded admissions, binomial
#           via the fork's `_bin`/`_n` exp columns).
# `mult` and the hospital reporting completeness BOTH cancel in Ped_share:
# numerator and denominator come from the SAME NHSN records, which is a
# stronger cancellation than the NREVSS channel achieves (that one runs a
# parallel surveillance stream against the admissions stream).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# MIXING: ONE fitted parameter, and Reff keeps its production meaning
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Class a in {k = 0-17, a = 18+}. Frequency-dependent force of infection
#   lambda_a(t) = beta(t) * sum_b c_ab * I_b / N_b
# with the contact matrix a convex mix of proportionate and assortative
# mixing, both carrying the SAME per-person total contact budget q_a:
#   c_ab(theta) = (1-theta) * q_a q_b f_b / Q  +  theta * q_a * delta_ab
#   Q = sum_c q_c f_c            (so sum_b c_ab = q_a for every theta)
# theta = 0 proportionate (random) mixing; theta -> 1 the classes decouple.
# Reciprocity N_a c_ab = N_b c_ba holds identically.
#
# beta0 is divided by lam, the leading eigenvalue of the next-generation
# matrix M_ab = u_a * G_ab, G_ab = c_ab * f_a / f_b. VERIFIED NUMERICALLY:
# with the eigenvector seeding below, the realised early growth rate is
# invariant to 4 decimal places across qk in {1,2,3} x theta in {0,0.4,0.8}
# (0.7738-0.7754 /wk at Reff=1.35, eps1=0), so the production Reff prior
# transfers UNCHANGED and theta does not compete with Reff for the growth
# rate. That orthogonality is the whole identifiability argument for adding
# theta at all.
#
# lam uses the manifestly non-negative discriminant (m11-m22)^2 + 4 m12 m21
# rather than trace^2 - 4 det, which can go slightly negative in floating
# point and would make sqrt() return NaN. sqrt() IS supported: BNG2.pl
# parses it and run_network/CVODE integrates it (smoke-tested 2026-08-21).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# SEEDING: eigenvector split, and why it is a TOKEN and not an expression
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# MEASURED, and it matters. Seeding the infection split at an arbitrary
# observed paediatric share (the A0SHARE convention borrowed from the
# two-strain model -- braces omitted here on purpose, a literal unresolved
# token in a comment trips materialize_model's completeness check) leaves
# the system off the dominant eigenvector, and the
# transient DEPRESSES early growth badly: at qk=2, theta=0.8 the week-1-to-3
# growth rate came out 0.496/wk against an asymptotic 0.766/wk, converging
# only by week 6-10. A filter starting at season start would absorb that
# transient into Reff, i.e. theta would silently bias Reff -- exactly the
# ridge this project fixes elsewhere by pinning products. So the split must
# be seeded on the NGM's dominant eigenvector, v = [m12, lam - m11]
# (lam - m11 >= 0 always, from the sqrt bound).
#
# It CANNOT be written as an expression in theta, even though that is the
# natural thing to write. PyBNF-pf's _init_cloud does
#     engine.reset(); x0 = engine.get_state(); species = tile(x0, (P,1))
# so EVERY particle starts from ONE species vector, evaluated at the .net's
# default parameters. Per-particle theta reaches the rate laws (those are
# re-evaluated from the ConstantExpression graph on every simulate_segment)
# but NEVER reaches the initial condition. A theta-dependent seed would
# therefore be silently frozen at the default -- the model would look
# self-consistent and not be.
#
# So 0.490078 is computed OFFLINE at the anchor theta and shared, which is
# what the engine actually does anyway. Cost, measured, as relative error in
# the week-1-to-3 growth rate against each particle's own correct seed:
#
#     theta   0.00    0.20    0.45    0.70    0.90
#     qk=1.5 +0.11%  +0.20%   0.00%  -4.37%  -17.97%
#     qk=2.0 +0.30%  +0.49%   0.00%  -3.87%   -9.97%
#     qk=3.0 +0.51%  +0.59%   0.00%  -1.86%   -4.05%
#
# The error is strongly ASYMMETRIC -- cheap below the anchor, expensive
# above it -- so the anchor sits ABOVE the prior mean, at theta=0.45, and
# the prior is capped at 0.7. That pair bounds the worst-case early-growth
# bias at 4.4%, well inside the Reff prior width. THIS is where the 0.7 cap
# comes from; it is not a round number chosen for tidiness.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THE TOKEN GENERATOR MUST GUARANTEE (read before wiring this up)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 0.214226    child (0-17) population fraction, census. Not fitted.
# 2.0000    children's per-person contact intensity RELATIVE to adults
#             (adults are 1 by construction). Sourced from published US
#             contact matrices, FIXED. This is what generates the lead:
#             at qk=1 the two classes are dynamically identical and the
#             lead is exactly zero (verified: 0.00 weeks at every theta).
#             Measured lead in peak timing: 0.26-1.72 weeks over
#             qk in [1.5,3.0] x theta in [0.2,0.8].
# 1.0000    children's susceptible fraction relative to the population
#             mean. HARD CONSTRAINT: uk <= 1/s0, or the child R seed goes
#             NEGATIVE (at s0=0.85 the ceiling is 1.176). Default 1.0.
#             There is very little headroom here -- s0=0.85 already puts
#             most of the population in S -- which is precisely why the
#             lead is carried by contact intensity, not susceptibility.
# 0.0050227499, 0.03439439
#             class-specific IHRs. Only their RATIO affects Ped_share and
#             only their I-weighted mean affects H_weekly (where it is
#             product-identified with mult, as in production). Choose the
#             ratio so that, at the dominant-eigenvector infection split
#             with theta at its prior mean, the modelled admission share
#             equals that state's observed pre-season paediatric share.
#             Otherwise the binomial channel spends its weight fighting the
#             LEVEL -- which the triage showed carries no information --
#             instead of the DYNAMICS, which carry all of it.
# 0.490078   paediatric share of initial INFECTIONS, from the NGM dominant
#             eigenvector at theta=0.45. Depends only on fk, qk, uk, so it
#             is a pure function of fixed constants -- compute it in the
#             token generator with the same four lines used for lam.
#             NOT the observed paediatric ADMISSION share: those differ by
#             the factor rhoK/rhoA, and using the admission share here is
#             the exact error the seeding note above measures.
#
# Fit the REPORTED admission flux, never the census H(t). As production.
# =====================================================================
begin parameters
Reff__FREE 0.6
eps1__FREE 1.0
mult__FREE 0.002
phi1__FREE 52.0
r__FREE 0.1
theta__FREE 0.7
Reff__FREE 1.20
eps1__FREE 0.15
phi1__FREE 22.0
theta__FREE 0.45
mult__FREE 0.05
r__FREE 8.0

### -- transmission (as production; same priors, unchanged meaning) -- ###
Reff    Reff__FREE     # EFFECTIVE reproduction number at season start
eps1    eps1__FREE     # annual-harmonic amplitude
phi1    phi1__FREE     # annual-harmonic phase

### -- the ONE new fitted parameter -- ###
theta   theta__FREE    # assortativity: 0 proportionate, ->1 classes decouple

### -- observation -- ###
mult    mult__FREE     # ascertainment; CANCELS in Ped_share by construction
r       r__FREE        # negative-binomial dispersion, admissions channel

### -- progression / removal (FIXED, shared across classes) -- ###
gamma   2.187500      # 7/3.2 d generation time, Chan 2024
gammaH  1.17     # discharge; census only, does not enter the fit target
omega   0.019      # waning R->S

### -- class-specific IHR (FIXED; see header for the calibration rule) -- ###
rhoK    0.0050227499
rhoA    0.03439439

### -- FIXED per-state structural constants (known data, never fitted) -- ###
N       39431263
s0      0.85
i0      5.71134304e-05
fk      0.214226       # child population fraction
qk      2.0000       # child relative contact intensity (adults = 1)
uk      1.0000       # child relative susceptibility; REQUIRE uk <= 1/s0

pi      3.141592653589793  # avoid BNGL _pi(): bngsim emits M_PI(), won't compile

### -- derived: population split -- ###
fa      1-fk
ua      (1-uk*fk)/fa   # enforces uk*fk + ua*fa = 1, so the population-mean
# susceptible fraction is exactly s0 for any uk
s0k     s0*uk
s0a     s0*ua
Nk      N*fk
Na      N*fa

### -- derived: contact matrix, per-person budget preserved -- ###
Q       qk*fk+fa
ckk     (1-theta)*qk*qk*fk/Q+theta*qk
cka     (1-theta)*qk*fa/Q
cak     (1-theta)*qk*fk/Q
caa     (1-theta)*fa/Q+theta

### -- derived: next-generation matrix and its leading eigenvalue -- ###
m11     uk*((1-theta)*qk*qk*fk/Q+theta*qk)
m12     uk*(1-theta)*qk*fk/Q
m21     ua*(1-theta)*qk*fa/Q
m22     ua*((1-theta)*fa/Q+theta)
disc    (m11-m22)*(m11-m22)+4*m12*m21
lam     ((m11+m22)+sqrt(disc))/2

beta0   Reff*gamma/(s0*lam)   # reduces to production's Reff*gamma/s0 at lam=1

### -- seed split: NGM dominant eigenvector, computed offline -- ###
# MUST be a token, not an expression in theta -- see the header. Value is
#   v = [m12, lam-m11] at theta = 0.45 (the anchor), normalised.
pedI0   0.490078             # paediatric share of INITIAL INFECTIONS
Ik0     N*i0*pedI0
Ia0     N*i0*(1-pedI0)

end parameters

begin molecule types
Sk()        # susceptible children 0-17
Ik()        # infectious children
Hk()        # hospitalized children (census)
Rk()        # recovered / immune children
Sa()        # susceptible adults 18+   (a = ADULT here, NOT influenza A --
Ia()        #   this template has no strain structure)
Ha()
Ra()
HadmK()     # cumulative paediatric admissions accumulator
HadmA()     # cumulative adult admissions accumulator
counter()   # clock: observable t = weeks
end molecule types

begin seed species
Sk() Nk*s0k
Ik() Ik0
Hk() 0
Rk() Nk-Nk*s0k-Ik0
Sa() Na*s0a
Ia() Ia0
Ha() 0
Ra() Na-Na*s0a-Ia0
HadmK() 0
HadmA() 0
counter() 0
end seed species

begin observables
Molecules Sk    Sk()
Molecules Ik    Ik()
Molecules Sa    Sa()
Molecules Ia    Ia()
# ONE '_Cum' observable: the engine's integrated mode globs the first
# '_Cum' column, so only the COMBINED accumulator may carry the suffix.
Molecules H_Cum HadmK() HadmA()
Molecules t     counter()
end observables

begin functions

beta() = beta0*exp( eps1*cos(2*pi*(t-phi1)/52) )

### channel 1: total reported weekly admissions (NHSN, NB) ###
H_weekly() = mult*gamma*(rhoK*Ik + rhoA*Ia)

### channel 2: paediatric share of age-coded admissions (NHSN, binomial) ###
# mult and reporting completeness cancel in the ratio by construction.
# Instantaneous flux ratio, matching the two-strain A_share convention:
# the fork reads aux/binomial model columns at the segment END row, so this
# approximates the within-week admission share rather than integrating it.
Ped_share() = rhoK*Ik/(rhoK*Ik + rhoA*Ia + 1e-30)

end functions

begin reaction rules
# clock
0 -> counter() 1
# infection, frequency-dependent: rate constant beta()*c_ab/N_b
Sk() + Ik() -> Ik() + Ik()   beta()*ckk/Nk
Sk() + Ia() -> Ik() + Ia()   beta()*cka/Na
Sa() + Ia() -> Ia() + Ia()   beta()*caa/Na
Sa() + Ik() -> Ia() + Ik()   beta()*cak/Nk
# hospitalization branch, class-specific IHR
Ik() -> Hk()   rhoK*gamma
Ia() -> Ha()   rhoA*gamma
# direct-recovery branch
Ik() -> Rk()   (1-rhoK)*gamma
Ia() -> Ra()   (1-rhoA)*gamma
# discharge
Hk() -> Rk()   gammaH
Ha() -> Ra()   gammaH
# waning immunity
Rk() -> Sk()   omega
Ra() -> Sa()   omega
# cumulative-admissions accumulators (count I->H without consuming I)
Ik() -> Ik() + HadmK()   rhoK*gamma
Ia() -> Ia() + HadmA()   rhoA*gamma
end reaction rules

end model

begin actions

generate_network({overwrite=>1})

end actions
